  data:  # 8956
    kind: invariant  # 8956
    title: "git notes have NEVER been pushed: pre-push applies the DCO check to refs/notes/*, which can never be signed, and the push swallows the failure"  # 8956
    status: needs_human_review  # 8956
    priority: 2  # 8956
    parent:  # 8956
    tags:  # 8956
    - governance  # 8956
    - ci  # 8956
    - data_integrity  # 8956
    isbefore: []  # 8956
    duplicate_of: []  # 8956
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 8956
    pr_ref:  # 8956
    description: ""  # 8956
    fields:  # 8956
      statement: "Commit bodies preserved via git notes must actually reach origin. They never have. refs/notes/commits holds 726 notes dating to 2026-01-22; git ls-remote origin shows ZERO notes refs on the remote. AGENTS.md documents recovery as 'git fetch origin refs/notes/*:refs/notes/*', which fetches nothing, and the bodies exist on exactly one machine."  # 8956
      root_cause: "Two defects compounding. (1) .githooks/pre-push loops over every ref being pushed and applies the DCO Signed-off-by check to all of them, with no exemption for refs/notes/*. Note commits are generated by git itself with the fixed message 'Notes added by git notes add' and cannot carry a sign-off; the DCO is a statement about contributing code, not about git's own bookkeeping commits. So every notes push is blocked. (2) scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh:2116-2117 pushes notes as 'git push origin refs/notes/commits --quiet 2>/dev/null || git push origin refs/notes/* --quiet 2>/dev/null || true' -- stderr suppressed and failure swallowed by || true, so the block has been invisible for nearly eight months. Even with (1) fixed, (2) would hide the next breakage."  # 8956
- op: discuss  # 6113
  at: '2026-09-12T19:00:53Z'  # 6113
  by: agent  # 6113
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6113
  clock: 2  # 6113
  nonce: '6113'  # 6113
  message: "OBSERVED 2026-09-12 when the human ran ./scripts/merge-pr 932. The PR MERGED FINE -- dev fast-forwarded to 8b80ba51f8 and the governance gate cleared. The red BLOCKED banner in that output is the notes push, not the merge, and it is a pre-existing condition rather than anything about PR #932.\n\nEVIDENCE:\n  git notes list                  -> 726 notes\n  git rev-list --count refs/notes/commits -> 731\n  oldest note commit              -> 2026-01-22\n  newest note commit              -> 2026-09-12 (PR #932)\n  git ls-remote origin | grep -c notes -> 0\n\nSo the mechanism has never once succeeded, from the day notes were introduced.\n\nBLAST RADIUS. AGENTS.md tells agents that historical commit bodies are recoverable via \"git fetch origin refs/notes/*:refs/notes/*\". That command currently fetches nothing. auto-pr --squash is documented as preserving the commit body via notes; that preservation is local-only. If this machine is lost, 726 commit bodies go with it. Nothing else depends on notes, so no analysis result is affected -- this is a durability and documentation-accuracy defect, not a correctness one.\n\nPROPOSED FIX, NOT STARTED -- BOTH FILES ARE GOVERNANCE FILES (.githooks/** and scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh), so per the AGENTS.md approval workflow this item is filed needs_human_review rather than PR-ed:\n\n(a) .githooks/pre-push -- skip the DCO block for refs under refs/notes/. Minimal and narrow: a guard on remote_ref, exempting nothing else. The protected-branch check above it is already inert for notes, since refs/notes/commits does not match refs/heads/*.\n\n(b) scripts/lib/forgejo-api.sh:2116-2117 -- stop swallowing the failure. Keep the push non-fatal (a notes push failing should not fail a merge that already landed) but report it on stderr instead of 2>/dev/null || true, so the next breakage is visible on the first occurrence rather than the 726th.\n\n(c) One-off backfill: push the existing 726 notes once (a) lands.\n\nOPEN QUESTION FOR THE OWNER, because it changes what (a) should say: is the DCO exemption correct in principle? My reading is yes -- the DCO certifies the origin of a CONTRIBUTION, and a notes-tree commit is git bookkeeping with an auto-generated message and no authored content. A reviewer who disagrees would instead want the notes mechanism retired and AGENTS.md corrected, which is a legitimate alternative outcome and is why this is not already a PR."  # 6113
